<http://data.silknow.org/object/0bbb1ee0-2b03-3b7a-ad85-11740a104564/observation/4>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "GOLD AND SILVER BROCADED SILKS \n\nVery few Indian textile-makers’ names are recorded, even when their works are collected for their supreme skill. The embroidered inscriptions on these pieces are a rare exception. The Persian script records that they are ‘the workmanship of Baks [Bakhsh] Miyan, resident of Kamalpura, for Mu’in Lal, resident of Gaighat’. These valuable gold and silver brocades (known as kamkhwab or kincob) were displayed in London at the Great Exhibition of 1851.\n\nSilk woven with gilded silver and silver-wrapped thread, embroidered with metal-wrapped silk thread\nVaranasi, Uttar Pradesh, about 1850\nV&A: 752A&B-1852 [03/10/2015-10/01/2016]"@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/0bbb1ee0-2b03-3b7a-ad85-11740a104564> .

